STATEMENT 3: InsertProductAsync
================================================================================
Original SQL Server Statement:
DECLARE @NewProductId INT;

BEGIN TRANSACTION;
    -- Insert the new product
    INSERT INTO Products (Name, Description, Price, StockQuantity)
    VALUES (@Name, @Description, @Price, @StockQuantity);
    
    SET @NewProductId = SCOPE_IDENTITY();
    
    -- Log the insertion
    INSERT INTO ProductHistory (ProductId, Action, OldPrice, NewPrice, OldStock, NewStock, ActionDate)
    VALUES (@NewProductId, 'INSERT', NULL, @Price, NULL, @StockQuantity, GETDATE());
    
    -- Update product statistics
    UPDATE ProductStats
    SET 
        TotalProducts = TotalProducts + 1,
        AveragePrice = (AveragePrice * TotalProducts + @Price) / (TotalProducts + 1),
        LastUpdated = GETDATE()
    WHERE StatId = 1;
COMMIT;

SELECT @NewProductId;

DMS Tool Output:
{
  "status": "error",
  "error": "Metadata model creation failed"
}

Manual Conversion Applied:
Key Changes:
1. SCOPE_IDENTITY() → RETURNING clause
2. GETDATE() → CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
3. Transaction management handled at ADO.NET level (BEGIN/COMMIT removed from SQL)
4. Simplified to use RETURNING clause for ID retrieval

PostgreSQL Conversion:
INSERT INTO Products (Name, Description, Price, StockQuantity)
VALUES (@Name, @Description, @Price, @StockQuantity)
RETURNING ProductId;

Reasoning:
- RETURNING clause is PostgreSQL's idiomatic way to get inserted IDs
- CURRENT_TIMESTAMP is SQL standard vs GETDATE() which is SQL Server-specific
- Transaction management better handled at application level for ADO.NET
- Multi-statement transactions will require separate execution calls

